In the model, why are there two levels of multi-normal? z0 and a0
are both simple vectors.

How did you get 1.06 out for a variable constrained to lower=0,upper=1?
Did you run the code as written?  If so, I have no idea how you got
1.06 out of a variable constrained <lower=0,upper=1>.  That would seem
to indicate some kind of bug in our transformed parameter block.
Do you have a model with data that replicates this behavior that you could
share?  Or even better, a simplified model with the same bad behavior.

I think the model would be cleaner with the 1..4 as array indices.
It'll cut down a lot on code duplication, which is a common source
of coding errors (not that I see any here).

You can define the identity matrix in the transformed data block
so that it's clear what it is reading the model:

transformed data {
  cov_matrix[noc] Imat;
  Imat <- diag_matrix(rep_vector(0,noc));
}

where I_matrix is K by K.  (If it's not always the identity, renaming would help.)
We really need an "id_matrix(K)" function --- the diag_matrix/rep_vector
composition is clunky.

Calling a covariance matrix "mu" is confusing to me.

- Bob

